    "samenvatting": "Recycling systems depend on the participation of citizens, businesses and organizations. Even established infrastructure can underperform when users do not know how to separate waste, cannot easily access collection services, perceive separation as inconvenient or expensive, or do not trust that collected materials are recycled effectively.",
    "toelichting": "",
    "waarom": "Recycling systems depend on the participation of citizens, businesses and organizations. Even established infrastructure can underperform when users do not know how to separate waste, cannot easily access collection services, perceive separation as inconvenient or expensive, or do not trust that collected materials are recycled effectively. Incorrect separation increases contamination, reduces recovery rates and material quality, and can increase collection and processing costs.\nParticipation is influenced by more than awareness. Practical barriers may include distance to collection points, limited opening hours, lack of space for separate containers, inaccessible facilities or unclear collection schedules. Financial barriers can include service charges, deposit arrangements or the costs and effort associated with transport. Social norms, language, trust, habits, housing type, organizational routines and the design of public space can also affect behavior.\nMunicipalities therefore need insights into the motivations, barriers and decision-making processes of different user groups and behavioral profile groups. This allows interventions to be tailored to specific neighborhoods, organizations, product streams and collection systems. Interventions may combine communication, service improvements, infrastructure changes, incentives, feedback and community engagement. Their effectiveness should be tested and monitored to ensure that they improve participation and material quality without creating unequal access or unintended consequences.",
