    "toelichting": "",
    "waarom": "Cities and regions contain significant stocks of materials embedded in buildings, infrastructure, vehicles, electronics, batteries, photovoltaic panels and technical installations. These urban stocks can be regarded as potential future sources of secondary materials. Urban mining is the systematic identification, mapping and recovery of valuable materials from products, buildings, infrastructure and waste streams within the urban environment.\nSome urban stocks contain Critical Raw Materials that are important for energy, mobility and digital technologies. When products reach the end of their use phase, these materials may be lost through inadequate collection, unsuitable processing, export or low-value treatment. Recovery may also be constrained by small and dispersed volumes, limited dismantling capacity, uncertain timing, insufficient information on material composition or the absence of technically and economically viable recovery routes.\nMunicipalities and regions need insight into which valuable and critical materials are present, where they are located, when they are expected to become available and which collection and recovery routes are feasible. This supports long-term infrastructure planning, cooperation with specialized processors and the development of regional urban-mining strategies. Where immediate recovery is not feasible, decision support may also consider whether products or components can be safely kept within controlled collection or storage systems until sufficient volumes, processing capacity or suitable recovery technologies become available. Any such temporary storage should have a defined purpose, responsible owner, safety conditions, maximum duration and intended processing route.",
